              {
                "text": "**Analyzing Row Heights**\n\nI've observed that the non-zero row pattern has a height of 3 (rows 4, 5, and 6). Specifically, in the first training set, rows 3, 4, and 5 contain non-zero values. The output from this training set replicates these three rows vertically, starting from row zero. Let me delve deeper into the row data to extract more complex patterns.\n\n\n**Refining Output Patterns**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on the output generation. It appears the output is a periodic tiling of the non-zero rows, but starting from the *pattern* row. Specifically, output[r] = input[r % 3 + pattern_row], where pattern_row is the initial row. The input pattern is at rows 4, 5, 6, I think I've solved this!\n\n\n**Discovering 2D Periodicity**\n\nI've discovered the patterns are not only vertically periodic, but 2D! The input grid shows repeating, periodic tiles. Specifically, I'm examining the horizontal patterns now. It seems that the rows in the input have repeating sections. The period in each row is the same as the vertical tiling height! It is not just about a vertical window, but a complete horizontal repeating tile.\n\n\n**Revisiting Periodicity Start**\n\nI'm now revisiting the periodicity and how it starts. I've realized the start row in the train data might be key. It looks like it is not simply the repeating tile, it is the start of the pattern. Thinking through the modulo arithmetic, it is like the pattern's period can have an offset, effectively starting at different points.\n\n\n**Defining Grid Generation**\n\nI'm now thinking about how to frame grid generation. It seems more natural to \"complete the periodic pattern\" across the grid. Train 2's diagonal structure is an important example; tiling it forms a continuous 2D pattern. For test inputs, I'm noting that a specific row in the output must match the same row in the input, as the grid is a window into the repeating pattern.\n\n\n**Confirming Input-Output Identity**\n\nI've confirmed that the output at any row r matches the input at row r, if the input row contained a pattern. This observation, seen in both train sets, disproves the initial hypothesis of tiling from row 0. Now I've completely proven output[r] = pattern[(r - 4) % 3], validating the pattern generation strategy. It is not possible to change the original non-zero pattern.\n\n\n",
